&lt;h4&gt;Why Kimchi?&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Great models are raw ingredients. Kimchi is what happens when you ferment them with good engineering. We started inside CAST AI helping companies run LLMs on their own Kubernetes clusters; today we run our own coding harness on any model you pick, and the router deciding which model answers each request is ours too.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Auto Router&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Our promise is one enforcement layer for your AI: budgets, spend attribution, and model routing, applied before a request ever hits a model. The Auto Router makes the call: for every request, which model serves it. Eight open-source models whose input prices span more than 20×, cached input up to 50× cheaper than fresh, frontier providers on customers&#39; own keys, and a coding agent that fires hundreds of requests before its task is done. Frontier-level task success at a fraction of the token cost: that sentence is the product, and it stays true only if the model inside the router keeps getting better. That model is yours.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em data-prosemirror-content-type=&quot;mark&quot; data-prosemirror-mark-name=&quot;em&quot;&gt;&lt;span id=&quot;edb2bdea-40e7-48f0-9485-a0bd7169c7c7&quot; data-mark-type=&quot;annotation&quot; data-mark-annotation-type=&quot;inlineComment&quot; data-id=&quot;edb2bdea-40e7-48f0-9485-a0bd7169c7c7&quot; data-prosemirror-content-type=&quot;mark&quot; data-prosemirror-mark-name=&quot;annotation&quot;&gt;Made &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;em data-prosemirror-content-type=&quot;mark&quot; data-prosemirror-mark-name=&quot;em&quot;&gt;&lt;span id=&quot;edb2bdea-40e7-48f0-9485-a0bd7169c7c7&quot; data-mark-type=&quot;annotation&quot; data-mark-annotation-type=&quot;inlineComment&quot; data-id=&quot;edb2bdea-40e7-48f0-9485-a0bd7169c7c7&quot; data-prosemirror-content-type=&quot;mark&quot; data-prosemirror-mark-name=&quot;annotation&quot;&gt;by Cast AI — a Miami-headquartered, globally distributed team across 34 countries that hit unicorn status in January 2026 with a $50B+ backed investment pushing its valuation past $1 billion.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;About the role&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Every request gets a decision: which model serves it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is an applied-ML seat, not an infrastructure one. Platform engineers own the proxy, the streaming path, and the fleet. You own the model inside them: the training data, the objective, the evaluation, and the iteration loop that ships a better router every cycle.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The problem:&lt;/strong&gt; routing is a prediction problem wearing a product&#39;s clothes. For every request you predict the quality each candidate model would deliver, what it would cost, and how fast it would decode, and you have to be right enough, in aggregate, that the savings are real and the quality never dips below frontier. The data is messy: rollout outcomes from benchmark harnesses, per-request records from the live fleet, override signals, and token streams whose speed depends on the serving concurrency at the moment. Speed itself is a correctness variable: on deadline-bound agentic work, a model that decodes three times slower doesn&#39;t do the same work slower, it does less work. That&#39;s why naive cost-per-token objectives quietly optimize for the wrong thing. And the bar for evidence is high: a router change ships only after production-parity evaluation with error bars, then gets held to its numbers in live traffic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Stack:&lt;/strong&gt; Python; PyTorch; SQL at scale (ClickHouse); OpenAI- and Anthropic-compatible LLM APIs; benchmark and rollout harnesses (RouterBench-class); GitLab CI.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Requirements&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You&#39;ve spent 5+ years in applied ML or ML research, and you&#39;ve trained, evaluated, and shipped models that make real-time decisions (ranking, recommendation, bandits, pricing, or LLM rout

&lt;h4&gt;Why Kimchi?&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Great models are raw ingredients. Kimchi is what happens when you ferment them with good engineering. We started inside CAST AI helping companies run LLMs on their own Kubernetes clusters; today we run our own coding harness on any model you pick, and we run the models underneath it ourselves.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kimchi Inference&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One API line and a customer&#39;s existing client just works: DeepSeek, GLM, Kimi, MiniMax, Nemotron served serverless from our own GPU fleet (OpenAI- and Anthropic-compatible, pay per token, spend visible per user, team, and key). When control matters, the same models run on the customer&#39;s own cluster instead. Behind that one line sits the machinery you&#39;ll own: SGLang and vLLM engines, B300 nodes, a shared cluster filesystem that holds every checkpoint exactly once, KV caches riding coding traffic with heavy prefix reuse, and the benchmark harness that gates every change we ship.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em data-prosemirror-content-type=&quot;mark&quot; data-prosemirror-mark-name=&quot;em&quot;&gt;&lt;span id=&quot;edb2bdea-40e7-48f0-9485-a0bd7169c7c7&quot; data-mark-type=&quot;annotation&quot; data-mark-annotation-type=&quot;inlineComment&quot; data-id=&quot;edb2bdea-40e7-48f0-9485-a0bd7169c7c7&quot; data-prosemirror-content-type=&quot;mark&quot; data-prosemirror-mark-name=&quot;annotation&quot;&gt;Made by Cast AI — a Miami-headquartered, globally distributed team across 34 countries that hit unicorn status in January 2026 with a $50B+ backed investment pushing its valuation past $1 billion.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;About the role&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Throughput. Latency. Cold starts. Cost per token.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Move those four numbers in the right direction and two things happen: customers get faster, cheaper inference, and our margins improve. That&#39;s the entire thesis of this role. Every loader you tune, every scheduler decision you land, every kernel you stare at shows up in a customer&#39;s p99 and on our P&amp;amp;L. Your mandate is the inference stack&#39;s optimization, whatever it takes, and the technical direction of how we optimize it is yours to argue with data.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The problem:&lt;/strong&gt; the checkpoints run 0.3-1.5 TB, and the &quot;right&quot; serving configuration depends on traffic shape, sequence-length distribution, batch dynamics, GPU SKU, memory bandwidth, and quantization tolerance, all variables that shift week to week. Most teams pick a configuration once, over-provision GPUs, and absorb the cost. We don&#39;t: we took Kimi-K3 (1.5 TB) from over an hour cold-to-serving to under twelve minutes with a shared filesystem, O_DIRECT weight loaders, and node-local JIT caches, and we&#39;re nowhere near done. Prefill/decode disaggregation and tiered KV caches are next.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Stack:&lt;/strong&gt; Python; SGLang; vLLM; TensorRT-LLM; PyTorch; fastsafetensors; CUDA-adjacent tooling; Kubernetes; virtiofs shared filesystems; ClickHouse; OpenTelemetry; Prometheus; Grafana; GitLab CI.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Requirements&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You&#39;ve built real ML systems for 5+ years, and your portfolio shows depth in inference or training infrastructure, not just model training notebooks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your Python is production-grade: services, not scripts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You&#39;ve run vLLM, SGLang, or TensorRT-LLM for real, and you have a working mental model of why an engine performs the way it does on a given GPU.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You&#39;ve measured quantization regressions, not just compression ratios.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multi-GPU and multi-node setups hold no fear: tensor and expert parallelism, collective communication, and their practical failure modes.&lt;/li&gt;
